  "headline": "Reflation with a tightening bias: yields at highs, copper firm, gold left behind",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as reflation the bond market is pushing back against. The 10-year yield sits at 5%, up 35 basis points over the month and at the top of its 52-week range, while copper is up 3.5% on the month and 19% year-to-date at 94% of its range. Growth-sensitive assets are firm and rates are rising with them: this is a strong-nominal-growth configuration, not a growth scare.",
    "The interplay confirms it. The copper/gold ratio is up 6.5% over the month, and gold has fallen 2.9% in the same window as yields rose, which is what real-rate pressure does to a non-yielding asset. Bitcoin outrunning gold by 29% over the month, and up 6% today, speaks to liquidity and risk appetite rather than fear. The curve tells the sharper story: the 30-year has risen only 14 basis points on the month against 35 for the 10-year, flattening the 30y-10y spread by 21 basis points. That bear flattening is a market pricing policy that stays tight into an economy running hot, not a term-premium blowout.",
    "The tension is energy and the dollar. Brent has shed 5% today and 5.4% on the week, trimming the inflation impulse even as it remains up 62% year-to-date, and yields still rose today with oil falling, so the rate move is about growth and policy rather than crude. The dollar index is up 1.1% on the week alongside yields, a rate-differential dollar rather than a haven bid, and it is straining the rupee and yen, both in choppy volatility regimes. Weight the yields and copper: they agree, and they are at the extremes."
